const title = 'Domestic Flights vs High-Speed Rail in China: Which Should You Choose?';
const description = 'Train or plane for your China trip? An honest, numbers-backed comparison — plus the overnight-sleeper trick that saves you an entire hotel night.';
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} lang="en">
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-8 not-prose">
      <a href="/en/transport/" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm text-gray-400 hover:text-gray-700 transition no-underline group">
        <svg class="w-3.5 h-3.5 transition-transform group-hover:-translate-x-0.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
          <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 19l-7-7 7-7"/>
        </svg>
        Back to Transport
      </a>
    </nav>
    <header class="mb-12">
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-3">🚄 Transport</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold text-gray-900 mb-4 leading-tight">
        Domestic Flights vs High-Speed Rail in China: Which Should You Choose?
      </h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 mb-6">Train or plane for your China trip? Here's the honest, numbers-backed answer — plus the overnight-sleeper trick that saves you an entire hotel night.</p>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 text-sm text-gray-500">
        <span>📅 Last updated: August 2026</span>
        <span>⏱️ 9 min read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="bg-gradient-to-r from-blue-50 to-indigo-50 border-l-4 border-blue-500 rounded-r-2xl p-8 mb-12 not-prose">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-blue-900 mb-4">🎯 Quick Answer</h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-blue-800 leading-relaxed">
        If the train ride is under 4.5 hours, <strong>take the train</strong>. You'll get there about as fast once you count the airport shuffle, the price won't jump around on you, and you skip the two-hour "be at the airport early" tax. Planes only pull ahead on the long hauls — Beijing to Chengdu, Shanghai to Kunming — or when you happen to catch a genuinely cheap last-minute fare. The classic case is Beijing–Shanghai: 4.5 hours on the train versus roughly 5.5 hours door-to-door by air, for about the same money or less. And if your budget is tighter than your schedule, there's a third option most guides skip entirely: the overnight sleeper, which quietly saves you a fare difference <em>and</em> a night of hotel. More on that below.
      </p>
    </div>

    <nav class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-8 mb-12 not-prose">
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">📋 In This Guide</h3>
      <ul class="space-y-3">
        <li><a href="#beijing-shanghai" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">🚄 Train or Plane on Beijing–Shanghai?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#three-rules" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">🧭 How Do You Decide? Three Rules of Thumb</a></li>
        <li><a href="#when-fly" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">✈️ When Does Flying Actually Make Sense?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#hidden-cost" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">💸 The Hidden Cost of Flying</a></li>
        <li><a href="#when-train" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">🚆 When Should You Just Take the Train?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#overnight-sleeper" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">🛏️ Want to Save Money? Take an Overnight Sleeper</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <div class="prose prose-gray max-w-none">

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-8">You don't need a booking walkthrough here — for that, see the <a href="/en/transport/high-speed-rail/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">High-Speed Rail Guide</a> and the <a href="/en/transport/transport-guide/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Transport Guide</a>. What you actually need is one answer: <em>for this trip, train or plane?</em> It's the first thing nearly every first-time visitor asks, and the honest answer is "it depends — but usually the train."</p>

      <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

      <h2 id="beijing-shanghai" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-8 flex items-center gap-3">
        <span class="text-4xl">🚄</span> Train or Plane on Beijing–Shanghai — Which Actually Wins?
      </h2>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-8">The busiest corridor in the country makes the whole argument for you:</p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-12 not-prose">
        <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse bg-white rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-gray-900 text-white">
              <th class="px-6 py-4 font-bold">Factor</th>
              <th class="px-6 py-4 font-bold">High-Speed Rail</th>
              <th class="px-6 py-4 font-bold">Flight</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-100 text-gray-700">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold">Travel time</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4">4h28m–6h (fastest G trains)</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4">~2h15m in the air</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-gray-50">
              <td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold">Door-to-door</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4">~5h (station is downtown both ends)</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4">~5.5–6h (airport + 2h early arrival)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold">Price (2nd class / economy)</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4">¥596–667, stable year-round</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4">¥400–1,200, volatile; doubles at holidays</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-gray-50">
              <td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold">Booking window</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4">15 days ahead; sells out at holidays, otherwise usually available</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4">Up to departure</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold">Hassle factor</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4">Low — off-peak security takes under 30 min</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4">High — 2h early, baggage fees on budget carriers</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-gray-50">
              <td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold">Reliability</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4">Very high — trains rarely cancel</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4">Weather delays are common</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

      <h2 id="three-rules" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-8 flex items-center gap-3">
        <span class="text-4xl">🧭</span> How Do You Decide? Three Rules of Thumb
      </h2>

      <div class="space-y-8 mb-12">
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">1️⃣ Under 4.5 hours by rail → just take the train</h4>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-gray-700">Beijing–Shanghai (4.5h), Beijing–Xi'an (4.5h), Shanghai–Hangzhou (45 min — barely worth the word "trip"), Chengdu–Chongqing (1h). There's no real debate.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">2️⃣ 4.5–6 hours → pause and compare</h4>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-gray-700">Beijing–Wuhan lands right here. The train usually still wins on total time, but glance at both — once in a while a flight edges it.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">3️⃣ Over 6 hours by rail → the plane earns its keep</h4>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-gray-700">Beijing–Chengdu (7.5h train vs 3h flight), Beijing–Guangzhou (8h rail), Shanghai–Kunming (11h rail). At that point the airplane gives you back half a day you'd rather spend doing something else.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

      <h2 id="when-fly" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-8 flex items-center gap-3">
        <span class="text-4xl">✈️</span> When Does Flying Actually Make Sense?
      </h2>

      <div class="space-y-8 mb-12">
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">🌏 The really long stuff</h4>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-gray-700">Beijing–Urumqi (12h+ by rail) or Shanghai–Kunming — a full day on the train just isn't worth it.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">💰 A cheap last-minute fare lands in your lap</h4>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-gray-700">On busy routes with several airlines (Beijing–Shanghai, Beijing–Guangzhou), off-peak economy can drop to ¥400–500, quietly undercutting second-class rail.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">🔗 You're feeding into an international connection</h4>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-gray-700">Flying to the hub city to catch your outbound flight often makes more sense than a long rail slog first.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">✈️ You want a direct route</h4>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-gray-700">Trains reach most cities, but sometimes only after a transfer or a long K/T/Z slog. If there's a non-stop flight and the rail option means switching trains mid-way, the plane wins on pure sanity.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

      <h2 id="hidden-cost" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-8 flex items-center gap-3">
        <span class="text-4xl">💸</span> The Hidden Cost of Flying (Read This Before You Book)
      </h2>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-8">A cheap airfare can fool you — the ticket isn't the whole price. Three things quietly add up:</p>

      <div class="space-y-8 mb-12">
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">🚇 Airports are far, even when they have a metro</h4>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-gray-700">Most big-city airports now have rail links (Beijing Capital's airport express runs ¥25, Shanghai Pudong is on Metro Line 2 plus the maglev, Guangzhou and Chengdu are on Lines 3 and 18) — but a metro line doesn't make the airport close. It still sits on the city's edge: ¥25–50 and 40–70 minutes each way, versus a train station downtown, reachable for ¥3–6 in 20–30 minutes. And in second- and third-tier cities, the airport often has no rail link at all — you're taking a taxi or shuttle.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">🧳 Check whether your fare includes a free checked bag</h4>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-gray-700">It's not just the budget carriers (Spring Airlines, West Air, 9 Air, Lucky Air, China United) — full-service airlines like Juneyao, China Eastern, and China Southern also sell promotional fares that leave out the checked bag. Read the fare rules before you click; a ¥50 "saving" can evaporate in a ¥150 baggage fee.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">⏰ Delays happen, and they snowball</h4>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-gray-700">Summer thunderstorms and winter fog regularly stack 2–4 hour delays on domestic routes. A delayed flight doesn't just eat your evening — it can make you miss your last metro or your connection.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-12">Add it all up before you compare: ticket + airport transfer + baggage + delay risk. Half the time the train was never more expensive — the math just looked like it.</p>

      <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

      <h2 id="when-train" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-8 flex items-center gap-3">
        <span class="text-4xl">🚆</span> When Should You Just Take the Train?
      </h2>

      <div class="space-y-8 mb-12">
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">🏙️ You're going city-center to city-center</h4>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-gray-700">Chinese airports sit 30–60 minutes outside town; train stations are usually right downtown. On Beijing–Shanghai, the train station is two metro stops from the Forbidden City; the airport is a 50-minute express ride out.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">🌧️ The weather's being dramatic</h4>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-gray-700">Winter fog in Chengdu, typhoon season down south — trains keep running when planes don't.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">🧳 You've got big bags</h4>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-gray-700">No baggage fees, no overhead-bin Olympics, just racks at the end of each carriage.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">👨‍👩‍👧 Traveling with kids or older family</h4>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-gray-700">Wider seats, easier boarding, and you can walk around whenever someone needs to stretch.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

      <h2 id="overnight-sleeper" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-8 flex items-center gap-3">
        <span class="text-4xl">🛏️</span> Want to Save Money? Take an Overnight Sleeper
      </h2>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-8">Everything so far has been about getting there <em>fast</em>. But if the budget is the boss, there's a third option that rarely makes it into the standard advice: the overnight sleeper on an old-school sleeper train.</p>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-8">Same idea as a red-eye flight, except Chinese sleeper trains are cheap enough that the math genuinely works. And don't overthink the letter: Z, T, and K-class sleeper fares are all distance-based, so on the same route the hard sleeper costs nearly the same no matter which letter — the real difference is time (Z-class fastest, then T, then K, roughly 12–16 hours on the Beijing–Shanghai overnight). So the practical rule is simple: among the overnight departures, take the <strong>fastest one that still leaves in the evening and arrives in the morning</strong>. You board, sleep, and wake up in a different city — having saved not just on the ticket itself but on a night of hotel you no longer need. One transaction, two savings.</p>

      <div class="space-y-8 mb-12">
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">🛏️ Hard sleeper (硬卧) — the budget pick</h4>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-gray-700">Open six-berth compartments, a firm mattress, no door, plenty of snoring. On the overnight Z/T/K runs, it comes to roughly ¥400–500 — undercutting second-class rail <em>and</em> a hotel room at once. This is the one that actually makes the backpacker math work.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">🛋️ Soft sleeper (软卧) — the comfort upgrade</h4>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-gray-700">Four berths behind a locking door, better pillows, far fewer disturbances. But the price climbs fast, and on some routes a soft sleeper costs about the same as a plane ticket — so check the fare before you romanticize it. It's comfort, not savings.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">🚄 Bullet-train sleepers (动卧)</h4>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-gray-700">Some overnight D-trains run with proper sleeper berths, so you keep the newer, smoother train and still get a bed, usually priced between hard and soft sleeper.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-4">A few practical bits from my own overnight runs:</p>

      <div class="space-y-8 mb-12">
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">1️⃣ On a budget? Choose hard sleeper — and pick your bunk wisely</h4>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-gray-700">The price difference between the top, middle, and bottom bunks is about 15 yuan per tier, with the bottom bunk being the most expensive and the most in demand. If you want to sit up in bed, go for the bottom bunk — it offers more space, so you can lean against the window when you're not sleeping, and there's a small table next to the bed for eating. However, the bottom bunk is more prone to odors from shoes and the restroom. If you value cleanliness, don't want strangers sitting on your bed, or prefer a quieter environment, choose the middle or top bunk. The middle bunk offers more space than the top bunk, but neither allows you to sit upright directly on the bed. However, if you choose the top or middle bunk, don't worry — there are folding chairs and tables along the aisle that you're free to use.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">2️⃣ Comfort first? Go soft sleeper</h4>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-gray-700">Each soft sleeper compartment has only four berths, offering more space and a quieter, more comfortable environment. Soft sleepers cost about 200 yuan more than hard sleepers.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">3️⃣ Pack earplugs, an eye mask, and slippers</h4>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-gray-700">Earplugs and an eye mask are non-negotiable, because the carriage never goes fully dark. I also recommend packing toiletries and slippers in your carry-on bag — they'll make your overnight train ride more comfortable and hygienic.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-12">When does it actually make sense? Look for routes with a <strong>10–15 hour overnight run</strong> on a Z- or T-class train — Beijing–Shanghai, Beijing–Xi'an, Shanghai–Guangzhou. Shorter than that and a daytime train gets you there in time for dinner anyway; longer than that and you're losing a full day regardless, at which point the flight starts looking like the sane choice again. And when two overnight options exist on the same route, just take the faster one — the fare is nearly identical either way.</p>

      <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

      <h2 id="booking" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-8 flex items-center gap-3">
        <span class="text-4xl">🎫</span> Where to Book Train and Flight Tickets
      </h2>

      <div class="space-y-8 mb-12">
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">🚄 Trains</h4>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-gray-700">Trip.com (English-friendly, small fee) or the official 12306 app. Tickets open 15 days ahead, and the good slots vanish fast — sleepers included; they're bookable the same way, just search the route and filter by "sleeper." See our <a href="/en/transport/high-speed-rail/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">high-speed rail guide</a> for the full walkthrough.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">✈️ Flights</h4>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-gray-700">Trip.com is the easy starting point — search your dates, pay with a foreign card in an English checkout, and the fare rules (including whether a bag is included) are shown before you pay. <a href="https://www.trip.com/t/yxIuEHgWsV2" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Search flights on Trip.com</a></p>
        </div>
